UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$The middle ear contains the three smallest bones in the body — the {{c1::malleus, incus, and }}{{c2::stapes}}{{c1:: (sometimes called hammer, anvil, and }}{{c2::stirrup}}{{c1::)}}. They amplify the vibrations from the tympanic membrane and transmit them to the inner ear; the base of the {{c2::stapes}} rests on the {{c3::oval window}} of the cochlea.$px$ WHERE id = '88a45af1-92c4-4200-b7d7-30b91ebd9106';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px${{c1::Franz Gall}} proposed one of the earliest theories that behavior, intellect, and personality might be linked to brain anatomy. His doctrine — {{c2::phrenology}} — argued that a well-developed trait would enlarge the corresponding brain region and produce a measurable bulge on the skull. The theory was later {{c3::falsified}}, but it foreshadowed the modern principle of regional brain specialization.$px$ WHERE id = '890a7452-a443-471d-845e-4d605a3de8b3';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px${{c1::Configurational isomers}} share the same connectivity but differ in their spatial arrangement in a way that cannot be undone by rotation alone — converting one to the other requires {{c2::breaking and re-forming covalent bonds}}. Enantiomers, diastereomers, and cis/trans isomers are all {{c1::configurational}}.$px$ WHERE id = '89965f67-ea31-41e1-8fc4-6f13d6fa31c9';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$A {{c1::polar covalent}} bond shares electrons {{c2::unequally}}: the more electronegative atom takes a partial negative charge (δ–) and the less electronegative atom takes a partial positive charge (δ+).$px$ WHERE id = '8a23920e-0d88-475b-8a8f-460407cf9366';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$For reactants and products in solution, reaction rate is reported in units of {{c1::M·s⁻¹}} (equivalently mol·L⁻¹·s⁻¹) — a molar concentration change divided by a time interval.$px$ WHERE id = '8a643b15-df61-43bc-972d-865893b3402a';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$Atoms tend to fill their valence shell with eight electrons — the {{c1::octet rule}}. Period 3 and below can {{c2::expand the octet}} (hold more than 8 valence electrons) by using their empty {{c3::d subshell}}, which is why sulfur and phosphorus form compounds like SF₆ and PF₅.$px$ WHERE id = '8ad08a45-51cd-4ebb-ba57-96afbd216490';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px${{c1::Calorimetry}} measures heat exchange with the environment, providing an empirical estimate of {{c2::basal metabolic rate (BMR)}} — the baseline energy expenditure at rest.$px$ WHERE id = '8b1c5e52-a1d3-40a9-b329-9c894e5e6426';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$The maximum electron capacity of a subshell is {{c1::4ℓ + 2}}, yielding 2 in s (ℓ=0), 6 in p (ℓ=1), 10 in d (ℓ=2), and 14 in f (ℓ=3) — the (2ℓ+1) orbitals per subshell times 2 electrons per orbital under Pauli.$px$ WHERE id = '8b2f4245-d9f3-4fc6-942a-44d4ffcd620e';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$The general molecular formula for a saturated, acyclic alkane is {{c1::CₙH₂ₙ₊₂}} — every additional carbon adds two hydrogens, reflecting the tetravalence of sp³ carbon.$px$ WHERE id = '8b4a36cd-90cb-491c-9e1c-a448ab256f56';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px$In {{c1::Hershey–Chase}}, bacteriophage DNA was radiolabeled with {{c2::³²P}} while the protein coat was labeled with {{c2::³⁵S}}; only DNA entered bacteria, identifying it as the genetic material.$px$ WHERE id = '8b8bae6a-2bc7-4bdf-aa6a-ad362178b6e2';
UPDATE public.flashcards SET cloze_text = $px${{c1::Effective nuclear charge (Zeff)}} is the net positive charge actually experienced by an atom's outermost electrons after accounting for {{c2::shielding}} by the inner-shell electrons — formally Zeff ≈ {{c3::Z − S}}, where S is the {{c2::shielding}} constant.$px$ WHERE id = '8c04acab-ca5b-4849-a165-5138bc535b4e';
